\subsection{Share Recipes without Reading Overwritten Homes}

A descriptor can store a graph of scalar computations rather
than one independent expression per local. This saves metadata
when several reconstructed values depend on the same optimized
temporary. Assume the scalar graph is acyclic, uses exact or
explicitly wrapping arithmetic as declared, and cannot fault.
Native inputs are literals or saved machine homes; those inputs
must be captured before allocating or writing the destination
frame. Here input homes hold only scalars, so no collector
participation is needed to capture them. Object materialization
still uses the distinct shell-and-fill procedure above.

\begin{sudocode}[Evaluate scalar nodes before entering the new frame]
\State validate graph, types and required input homes
\State $capture\gets$ copy of all referenced scalar homes
  \Comment{Native and destination storage may overlap}
\ForAll{scalar recipe nodes $v$ in dependency order}
  \If{$v$ denotes a home or constant}
    \State $result[v]\gets$ captured value or literal
  \Else
    \State $result[v]\gets$ evaluate using operand results
      \Comment{One evaluation per node}
  \EndIf
\EndFor
\State populate canonical scalar slots from $result$
\State resume at descriptor's PC
\end{sudocode}

Suppose native homes $r_0=6,r_1=4$ survive an optimized
branch. A recipe computes $t=r_0+r_1$ and restores local
0 to $t$, local 1 to $2t$, and local 2 to the original
$r_0$. The canonical values are $(10,20,6)$. If the
runtime reuses the memory cell holding saved $r_0$ for local
0, writing that slot early and then reading the old home
would yield $(10,20,10)$. A fixture can deliberately arrange
the overlap. Add a self-dependent scalar recipe as a negative
test: reject it even though cycles in the virtual object graph
are legal.

The invariant is that each scalar result denotes the recipe's
value on the machine state at the exit, not on a partly
rebuilt frame. Dependency-ordered evaluation takes linear
work in nodes and edges. Stateful loads and effects are not
scalar recipes; recovery must use a previously saved result
or choose another valid continuation. Bound the graph size so
an uncommon exit does not conceal unexpectedly expensive recovery.

\section{Replay needs more than deoptimization}

Dossier Chapter~04 proposes explicit deoptimization operations, and the system blueprint checks their recipes before publication. Dossier Chapter~35 proposes extending recovery to checkpointed deterministic replay. This is additional machinery, not a property of ordinary deoptimization or of dispatched specialized continuations in Fl\"uckiger and colleagues' \emph{Deoptless}~\cite{fluckiger2022deoptless}. The latter provides a faster kind of forward continuation under appropriate contexts, not a time machine.

In the example, a checkpoint before \texttt{inner} must retain the guest heap state, logical stack and semantic revision, plus the next event cursor. The log stores the read result 42 at that cursor. Restore a private copy, feed the result through a replay stub and send writes to a validating sink. Compare the reconstructed PC, alias graph and observation prefix to the saved failing state, checking that replay itself made zero real device calls. For edits and code retirement (Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-259}), the log must pin or otherwise retrieve the version metadata it promises to use. A missing event must stop replay rather than silently consulting the device. With threads, deterministic replay additionally needs the relevant schedule or memory-order decisions; the single-thread example makes no such guarantee. Measure event volume and checkpoint retention separately from the cost of normal guard exits.
